  "name": "The Princess Is Not In Danger",
  "author": "",
  "proseAuthor": "dinniman",
  "narratorVoice": "",
  "tone": "high",
  "startingLocation": "Little Piddle-on-the-Marsh",
  "startingRegion": "The Kingdom of Gorse — three towns, one forest that argues, one mountain of no particular importance, and a great many sheep",
  "startingTime": "",
  "premise": "The Kingdom of Gorse has a prophecy, a princess, and a dragon, and for the first time in its history it has all three at once. The prophecy, delivered by the Oracle of Gorse after what witnesses describe as a substantial lunch, says that the princess shall be carried off by a dragon and rescued by a stranger in a hat before Thursday. Grunwald the Considerate, the only dragon in the kingdom and by common consent the politest creature in it, read the prophecy in the newspaper and carried Princess Wilhelmina Underbough off the next morning, apologising throughout. He has been reading her his memoirs ever since. The princess is not in danger. She is, according to the letters she sends home by griffin, extremely bored, and would like someone to come and get her before the third volume. The King has hidden the letters, because a rescue is Tradition and Tradition does not have a chapter for a princess who is not in danger. Every knight in Gorse has found a reason to be elsewhere. Nobody knows what day it is, because the monks of Great Snoring stopped ringing the calendar bell in protest over something nobody remembers. You are the stranger. You are, at the moment the herald finds you, wearing a hat.",
  "acts": [
    {
      "title": "Everyone Else Was Busy",
      "goal": "Be chosen by the prophecy in Little Piddle-on-the-Marsh, learn where the dragon actually lives from the one person who knows and will not say, and leave town by griffin before the town changes its mind",
      "turningPoint": "The postal griffin delivers you, and the King's hidden letters, to the doorstep of Mildew Hall — and you learn from the princess's own hand that she is bored rather than imperilled, that the deadline is Thursday, and that nobody in Gorse can tell you what day it is",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "The Hat",
          "objective": "Survive being identified as the stranger in a hat by Herald Fennimore Bracket in front of the whole of Little Piddle, and accept the rescue with as much dignity as the situation permits",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Fennimore is a halfling of enormous, unshakeable dignity who has never once been in on a joke. The town treats the prophecy with the gravity of weather. The player's hat is examined by a committee. Nobody asks whether the player can fight; they ask whether the hat is the player's own. Play it all completely straight — the comedy is that nobody here finds any of it funny."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Marrow Question",
          "objective": "Get the way to the dragon's mountain out of Gudrun Tibbs, the dwarf marrow-grower who knows it and will not speak until the Annual Marrow has been judged — by you",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "The Marrow is the most important thing that happens in Little Piddle and has been for four hundred years. There are nine entries, two feuds, one alleged sabotage, and a marrow that may be a pumpkin. Gudrun's rivals will try to influence the judge. Every conversation is about the marrow first and the dragon second, if at all. Judge wrongly and the town turns; judge rightly and Gudrun gives directions so precise they include what to say to the trees."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Griffin Post",
          "objective": "Persuade Percival, the Royal Postal Griffin, to carry a passenger, which he does not do, and be carried",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "Percival is a magnificent, exhausted civil servant with wings. He carries letters. He does not carry people; people are not letters. The solution is to become, technically, post — a limerick address, the right kind of ribbon, being addressed to the correct mansion. In flight he confides that he has been carrying the princess's letters home for six weeks and the King keeps them in a drawer. He lands at Mildew Hall because that is where the next letter goes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Mildew Hall Players",
      "goal": "Get through the haunted mansion of Mildew Hall, whose ghosts are not restless so much as under-stimulated, and come out the other side with the safe way through the Wood That Disagrees — which the ghosts know and will only reveal to members of the company",
      "turningPoint": "Opening night is a triumph, in the sense that the audience is dead and the reviews are savage but everyone survives, and Lady Ottoline Frisk hands over the only map of the Wood that the Wood has ever agreed to — along with the news that the dragon believes himself cursed, and that the curse is a remark somebody once made to him at a party",
      "parallel": true,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Auditions",
          "objective": "Enter Mildew Hall, be mistaken for the new cast, and secure a part in Lady Ottoline Frisk's production of a play she has been rewriting for two hundred years",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "The ghosts of Mildew Hall died of nothing in particular and have taken up amateur theatre to pass eternity. The house is genuinely eerie — cold spots, doors, a dinner nobody eats — and every haunting turns out to be a rehearsal. Lady Ottoline is a director in the terrifying sense. Casting is decided by the player's ability to project, not to fight. Brisa the Understudy, a ghost who has waited a century for a lead, is the player's only ally and worst danger."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Second Act Problem",
          "objective": "Solve the mystery at the heart of the play — how Colonel Augustus Frisk actually died in 1712 — because the Colonel has forgotten, the play cannot end until he remembers, and the Colonel is in the cast",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "A locked-room mystery investigated by interviewing the victim, who is helpful, vague and eager. Clues are props. The house replays the night if asked nicely. The answer is undignified (he was not murdered; the case for murder was made by Lady Ottoline because it was a better second act). The Lantern of Mild Suspicion hangs in the props cupboard: held up, it makes the nearest lie feel faintly embarrassed, and glows a bit."
        },
        {
          "title": "Opening Night",
          "objective": "Perform the play to a house of the dead without the manticore in the orchestra pit stopping the show, and survive the reviews",
          "type": "combat",
          "dnaHint": "Lionel the manticore was hired as an orchestra and stayed as a heckler; he is a failed poet and a savage critic, and his spines are real. The bout is a performance — the player fights and acts at once, the audience throws things it can no longer pick up, and the ghosts' notes afterwards are the true test of courage. A player who takes a note gracefully is made a member of the company for life, which is the point."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Wood That Disagrees",
      "goal": "Cross the ancient forest, whose trees argue about which way is north, pass the troll's bridge quiz and the unicorn's standards, fit spectacles to a near-sighted basilisk, and find out from the town of Great Snoring what day it is",
      "turningPoint": "In Great Snoring, where the monks communicate entirely by eyebrow, you finally establish that Thursday is tomorrow — and the Oracle of Gorse, found asleep in the monastery orchard, admits that the prophecy was her shopping list, that the word was not dragon but dragon-fruit, and that she has never been able to read her own handwriting after lunch",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Directions",
          "objective": "Get from one side of the Wood That Disagrees to the other, which requires the trees to agree, which they will not",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "The oaks say north is one way, the beeches another, and a single elderly yew has opinions about both. Paths rearrange according to the argument. Gudrun's directions (what to say to the trees) work if repeated exactly, including the compliments. The Wood is beautiful, old, and petty. The Ring of Knowing Roughly, found in a hollow stump, points somewhat north-ish and hums when it is being ignored."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Bridge Quiz",
          "objective": "Cross the troll's bridge by answering three questions the troll wrote himself, all of which are about the troll",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Keith the troll gave up the traditional arrangement years ago because nobody enjoyed it. Now there is a quiz. The questions concern Keith's childhood, Keith's favourite colour, and what Keith said to a dragon at a party in 1804, which Keith regrets. The player can research the answers from the trees, who remember everything and disagree about all of it. A wrong answer is not fatal; Keith just looks disappointed, which is worse."
        },
        {
          "title": "A Basilisk Wearing Spectacles",
          "objective": "Deal with Doris, the near-sighted basilisk who has been turning the forest's squirrels partly to stone by accident, before she does it to the party",
          "type": "combat",
          "dnaHint": "Doris cannot see past the end of her snout and petrifies whatever she squints at, which is everything. Half the squirrels in the Wood have one stone leg. The fight is real — her gaze is a weapon that does not know it is one — and the solution is a pair of spectacles from Dunmore the gnome optician, who lives in the Wood because nobody else will have him. A fitted Doris is the most grateful creature in Gorse and follows the party for a day."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Unicorn's Standards",
          "objective": "Cross the clearing of Ambrose the unicorn, who admits only the pure of heart, has never met one, and is not about to start",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Ambrose is a snob of the highest order and entirely sincere about it. He finds fault with everyone: posture, motives, hats. He cannot be fought and will not be fooled; he can be bored, flattered into contradiction, or — the real key — asked about himself. He is the one who tells the party what Keith said to the dragon at the party, because Ambrose was there, and he has been dining out on it for two centuries."
        },
        {
          "title": "Great Snoring",
          "objective": "Enter the silent town of Great Snoring, find out what day it is from monks who have not spoken in a decade, and wake the Oracle",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "The monks of Great Snoring took a vow of silence over a dispute nobody remembers and have refined communication by eyebrow to an art. The calendar bell hangs unrung. Charades with Brother Osric, an orc of great gentleness and one expressive eyebrow, establishes the day. The Oracle, Mad Dot, an elf of tremendous age and appetite, sleeps in the orchard with her cat; woken, she is unrepentant. Thursday is tomorrow. The mountain is a day away. Someone will have to go faster than that."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Rescue Optional",
      "goal": "Qualify at the Rescue Trials in the arena at Hobb's End, climb Mount Considerable, and get the princess out of Grunwald's memoirs before Thursday — or find a better ending than the prophecy's",
      "turningPoint": "At the top of the mountain, Grunwald the Considerate explains, with great courtesy, that he cannot release the princess until the prophecy is fulfilled or the curse is lifted, and the princess explains, with less courtesy, that the curse is that Keith the troll once told him at a party that nobody would ever want to hear his memoirs — and the player realises they know an entire theatre company of the dead who are desperate for new material",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Hobb's End",
          "objective": "Reach the arena town at the foot of the mountain, register for the Rescue Trials by tomorrow morning, and discover that the town's only other industry is cheese",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Hobb's End is entirely organised around two things: the Trials, held whenever a princess is taken, and cheese, held constantly. Mayor Bettany Rind, a half-elf of relentless civic pride, will explain both at length. The Boots of Modest Speed are the Trials' traditional loan to entrants: the wearer arrives everywhere ten minutes earlier than they should and can never explain how. The Helm of Overwhelming Confidence is on sale in the market and should not be worn near the hydra."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Rescue Trials",
          "objective": "Win the arena — against the Hydra Committee, whose five heads must all agree before it can bite, and against Sir Wendel the Undefeated, aged eighty-one — without hurting anybody's feelings, which is the rule",
          "type": "combat",
          "dnaHint": "The Trials have been non-lethal by tradition since the incident nobody discusses. The hydra is a real and dangerous animal whose heads hold a vote before every strike; a player who can get one head to abstain is unbitten. Sir Wendel has been champion for forty years because every opponent forfeits rather than hurt an old man's feelings; the crowd expects the player to do the same, and the crowd is not wrong. The Sword of Reasonable Doubt, the Trials' prize, is a good blade that is never certain it hit anything and says so."
        },
        {
          "title": "Mount Considerable",
          "objective": "Climb the mountain in the Boots of Modest Speed and arrive at the cave in time, or ten minutes early, whichever the boots decide",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "The mountain is of no particular importance and knows it. The path is well kept, because Grunwald keeps it. There are signs. The signs apologise for the gradient. Doris the basilisk, if she is still following, must be kept facing downhill. The cave mouth has a mat."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Third Volume",
          "objective": "Meet Grunwald the Considerate, sit through as much of the memoirs as courtesy requires, and find the ending in which the princess leaves, the dragon is heard, and the prophecy is technically satisfied",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Grunwald is a large, red, endlessly apologetic dragon who has been waiting his whole life for a reader. Princess Wilhelmina is sharp, tired, halfway through a crossword, and entirely capable of walking out, which she has not done because it would be rude. Fire is possible if the player is rude first; nobody in this story has been rude first yet. The real solution is a booking: the Mildew Hall Players want a script, the dragon wants an audience, and the princess wants to go home by Thursday. The Cloak of Slight Invisibility, folded on the dragon's hoard, renders only the wearer's left elbow unseen and is offered as a parting gift with great ceremony."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Return",
          "objective": "Bring the princess home to Little Piddle-on-the-Marsh before the bell that nobody rings, and let her deal with her father",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Percival the griffin will carry her because she is, technically, post: she addresses herself. The King has the letters in a drawer and the excuse that it was Tradition; Wilhelmina has a great deal to say about Tradition. The player is thanked with the sincere, bewildered gratitude of a kingdom that never quite understood what it asked for. Somewhere behind them, in a cave with a mat at the door, a dragon is reading the first line of volume one to an audience of the dead, and it is going well."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
