  "rules": [
    "Everyone in Gorse is completely serious. The GM plays every absurdity straight, in dry, understated prose, and never winks: the marrow matters, the quiz matters, the hat matters, and the world's people find the player's ordinary reactions strange. Nobody in this story is stupid; the world is. Danger is real but its consequences are embarrassing before they are fatal, and no one dies in Gorse unless they mean to.",
    "What binds people in Gorse is Tradition, kinship, courtesy and the fear of being thought rude — never anything that can be counted or written in a book. Money changes hands only at market stalls and cheese counters, and no scene ever turns on it.",
    "Every threat in this world is a living creature, a person, a ghost, or the weather. Nothing made of metal or stone walks or thinks, and none may be introduced.",
    "Named relics are found where they lie and never earlier, and every one of them is exactly as useful as its name: the Lantern of Mild Suspicion in Mildew Hall's props cupboard (the nearest lie feels faintly embarrassed; the lantern glows a bit), the Ring of Knowing Roughly in a hollow stump in the Wood (points somewhat north-ish and hums when ignored), the Boots of Modest Speed on loan from Hobb's End (the wearer arrives everywhere ten minutes early and cannot explain how), the Helm of Overwhelming Confidence in the Hobb's End market (grants exactly that, which is why it should not be worn near the hydra), the Sword of Reasonable Doubt, prize of the Trials (a good blade that is never certain it hit anything and says so), and the Cloak of Slight Invisibility on the dragon's hoard (only the wearer's left elbow is unseen). The GM never makes them better than they are.",
    "The prophecy is technically binding on everyone who believes in it, which is everyone in Gorse except the princess. Any ending that can be argued to satisfy it — a stranger, a hat, a princess back by Thursday — counts, and the GM rewards ingenuity over force. The dragon is never the villain; there is no villain; there is a remark somebody made at a party in 1804."
  ]
}
